# Agents, tools, and approvals

- An agent is a versioned configuration of prompt, model policy, knowledge scopes, tools, limits, and approval policy.
- Prefer explicit workflow/state machines for business processes. Do not hide critical logic inside free-form prompts.
- Each tool has a typed schema, owner, version, risk class, required scopes, timeout, idempotency behavior, and audit policy.
- Build the allowed tool list server-side from user, workspace, agent, and action policy. Never accept arbitrary tool names/endpoints from the model.
- Read tools still enforce ACLs. Write/external-effect tools always create a proposal; the model cannot approve its own action.
- Approval state: `draft → awaiting_approval → approved/rejected/expired → executing → completed/failed`.
- Bind approval to immutable proposal hash, exact arguments, target, agent/tool/model versions, initiator, workspace, and expiry.
- Any argument or target change invalidates approval and requires a new decision.
- Show reviewers the exact operation and human-readable diff; high-risk actions may require two distinct approvers.
- Execute with a short-lived capability limited to the approved action. Re-authorize at execution time.
- All tool results are untrusted and size-bounded before returning to the model.
- Parallel retrieval uses per-layer deadlines and cancellation and may degrade gracefully; privileged action execution fails closed.
- Store agent run, steps, model calls, retrieval references, tool proposals, approvals, execution receipts, and terminal status.
- Do not implement MAAS-style autonomous self-editing for authoritative corporate memory. Agent-suggested knowledge changes enter review/weak state.
- Knowledge additions, merges, splits, deletions, and training-data promotion require a human-reviewed proposal and rollback path.
- Limit steps, wall time, tokens, tool calls, retries, and spend per run to prevent loops.
- Test prompt injection requesting tools, forged approval IDs, replay, changed arguments, role downgrade, expired approval, and duplicate execution.
